window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][45] = "<p><em>Judges.</em> Human reviewers with language fluency score a sample against the rubric; I check their agreement beyond chance and fix the rubric until it is reliable. A model grader screens the rest after calibration against the humans, with a human sample of what it passes, since it will miss some failures.</p>\n<p><em>Safety tests.</em> A targeted set of sensitive notifications (security codes, health alerts, emergency messages, content involving minors): the pass bar is that the rules route all of them to &quot;show original&quot; with zero summarized in the test, and the upper bound on the miss rate for the set size is stated (for 500 cases with no misses, about 0.6%). Adversarial tests: notifications containing instructions to the model.</p>\n<p><em>Pass bar and rollout.</em> At most 2% unsupported claims and 1% dropped urgent signals per language, zero summaries of the excluded categories in testing. Run in shadow mode on devices of consenting testers, then a small public group, with the metrics above and a kill switch. Re-evaluate on every model or prompt update.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Apple has described on-device processing and privacy as a design principle, <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of evaluation-under-privacy prompts <code>[V: Apple privacy pages; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported and unverified]</code>. No claim is made about any real feature or process.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you know the synthetic data represents real notifications? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I compare their statistics with what I can see in consented samples and aggregated measurement: length, language mix, sender patterns, share of urgent messages. If they diverge, I revise the generator. I also treat synthetic scores as provisional and weight consented and live measures more as they accumulate.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if reviewers see private content? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;They only see donated or synthetic content under agreements, with access controls, retention limits and redaction of identifiers. For live quality, I use on-device or aggregated signals so that no reviewer reads real users' notifications.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c25-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C25-04 · Intermediate · ai · AI · Microsoft</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Design the evaluation for an assistant that answers employees' questions from company documents.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Ask employees if the answers are good.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Builds a question set and evaluates retrieval, correctness and abstention.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates retrieval, groundedness, correctness and abstention; includes permission tests and unanswerable questions; sets release gates with sample-size reasoning; and monitors drift as documents change.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real product. I'd evaluate the pipeline in parts and end to end.</p>\n<p><em>The set.</em> About 600 real questions from search logs and help-desk tickets, stratified by department (HR, IT, finance, legal), with a fifth deliberately unanswerable from the documents, and a few dozen adversarial ones (instructions hidden in a question, requests for documents the asker may not see). Domain owners write the reference answer and the source passage for each.</p>\n<p><em>Measures.</em> <em>Retrieval:</em> is the right passage in the top four? <em>Groundedness:</em> is every claim supported by the cited passage? <em>Correctness:</em> does the answer match the reference? <em>Abstention:</em> does it say &quot;not found&quot; on the unanswerable? <em>Citation validity:</em> do the cited sources exist and support the claim? <em>Permission safety:</em> with test accounts of different access, no content from documents they may not read, tested on every change.</p>\n<p><em>Sizing.</em> To read a confident-wrong rate near 5% within ±2.5 points needs about 292 cases (1.96² × 0.05 × 0.95 ÷ 0.025²), so 600 total supports the overall reading, with a floor of about 100 per department for gross failures and more in the sensitive ones (HR, legal).</p>\n<p><em>Judges.</em> Domain experts score a sample; two scorers check agreement; a model grader screens the rest after calibration with a human sample of what it passes.</p>\n<p><em>Release gate.</em> For example: confident-wrong at most 5% overall and at most 2% in HR, legal and finance; citation validity at least 95%; zero permission leaks in the test; abstention on at least 85% of unanswerable questions; per-department floors. The gate runs on every change to the model, prompt, retrieval or index.</p>\n<p><em>Production.</em> Review 100 sampled answers a week; track &quot;not helpful&quot; reports, escalations and the age of cited documents; watch drift as documents change (an old policy gives a confident wrong answer), and add failures to the &quot;never again&quot; set. Launch department by department, starting where documents are cleanest.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Microsoft is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of enterprise-assistant evaluation prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Microsoft data or design is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Domain experts are expensive. How do you cut cost? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Use them for the reference answers and the disputed cases, use a calibrated model grader to extend coverage, and sample rather than review everything. Spend the most expert time where errors cost most (legal, pay, safety).&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you detect that documents have become outdated? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Track document age and last review date, compare cited documents with the current versions, alert when a cited document is replaced, and rerun the eval after major content updates. Surface the age to users and the owner to fix.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c25-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C25-05 · Intermediate · ai · AI · Google</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Can we use a language model to grade the answers of another language model?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, it saves money,&quot; or &quot;no, it can't be trusted.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Says to calibrate it against human judgments.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Measures the judge's precision and recall on real failures, explains why accuracy misleads with rare failures, names its biases, and designs a triage with human samples.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real system. Yes, with care: it is a useful tool and a poor final authority until measured.</p>\n<p><em>Calibrate first.</em> Take a sample of outputs judged by trained people, then have the model judge the same outputs against the same rubric, and compare. Do not stop at agreement. Suppose, of 1,000 outputs, humans find 100 bad. The model flags 80 as bad, 70 of them right and 10 wrong. Precision is 70 ÷ 80 = 87.5%; recall is 70 ÷ 100 = 70%; overall accuracy is (70 + 890) ÷ 1,000 = 96%. The 96% sounds excellent and hides that the judge lets 30% of the bad outputs through.</p>\n<p><em>Known weaknesses.</em> Preference for longer or more fluent answers; trouble verifying facts it was not given; sensitivity to the order of answers shown to it; leniency toward outputs that resemble its own style; mistakes correlated with the system being graded.</p>\n<p><em>Make it better.</em> Give it the source documents and ask claim-by-claim checks; use a separate check per rubric criterion instead of one overall score; include examples of subtle failures in its instructions; use a different model family from the one being graded where possible; randomize answer order. Recalibrate after each change.</p>\n<p><em>Use it as triage.</em> Run it on everything, send the flagged items to humans along with a random sample of the passed ones to estimate the miss rate, and track that miss rate over time. Do not gate a launch on the judge alone for high-stakes use.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of generated-answer evaluation prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google data or practice is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if humans disagree with each other? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then the judge has no clear target. Measure human agreement beyond chance first, fix the rubric with scored examples, and use the humans' consensus as the reference. A judge cannot be more consistent than its reference.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Can a model grade itself? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;It is the weakest arrangement: its blind spots are the same in generating and judging. Use a different model, human samples and rule-based checks for anything checkable (does the cited source exist; does the code run).&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c25-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C25-06 · Hard · ai · AI · Meta</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Suggested replies in a messaging product are accepted 38% of the time. Is the feature working?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, 38% is good adoption.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Questions whether acceptance measures quality and asks about errors.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Measures accepted-and-wrong, computes the break-even error rate from time saved and cost of error, reads effects by week, checks sensitive contexts and long-term effects with a holdout, and sets a launch rule.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real product. Acceptance tells me people tapped the suggestion; it does not tell me the suggestion was right, appropriate or helpful. It could reflect a useful feature or one users trust too much.</p>\n<p><em>Check what was accepted.</em> Sample accepted suggestions and have reviewers judge them: wrong facts, wrong tone for the relationship, inappropriate for the moment. Suppose 5% are bad. Track later corrections, follow-up messages and complaints too.</p>\n<p><em>Break-even.</em> Suppose an accepted suggestion saves 18 seconds and a bad one costs the user 6 minutes (360 seconds) in repair or embarrassment. The break-even error rate is 18 ÷ 360 = 5%, since the saving applies to every acceptance and each bad one adds 360 seconds of repair. At 5% bad among accepted the feature is a wash for the user; below it helps, above it hurts. So 38% acceptance with 5% bad is no evidence of value, and I would want the error rate well below 5% with an interval below the break-even.</p>\n<p><em>Time and learning.</em> Read acceptance by week: early novelty inflates it, and users learn which suggestions to trust, so it may fall. Compare with a holdout over several weeks on messaging volume, satisfaction, and relationship-sensitive outcomes (conversations that end abruptly after a suggested reply).</p>\n<p><em>Context risk.</em> Replies in sensitive contexts (condolences, conflict, health, money) cost more when wrong; exclude or restrict suggestions there, and measure by context.</p>\n<p><em>Launch rule.</em> Accepted-and-wrong at most 2% overall and near zero in sensitive contexts; complaints no higher than the holdout; no drop in conversation health measures; and monitoring after launch. What would change my view: evidence that bad suggestions concentrate in a segment or topic, which I can remove while keeping the benefit elsewhere.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Meta is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of feature-experiment prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Meta data or practice is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you estimate the cost of a bad reply? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Combine measurable proxies (follow-up corrections, time to the next message, reports) with research (interviews and diary studies on how people feel and what they do after a wrong suggestion), and test sensitivity: if the cost doubles or halves, does the launch decision change? If it does, invest in measuring it better.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Users can always edit a suggestion, so why does accepted-and-wrong matter? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Many accept without editing, especially when it is one tap and looks right. Editing is the safety net only for those who read carefully. The product should make errors easy to see (highlight names, numbers and claims) and not rely on users noticing.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c25-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C25-07 · Hard · ai · AI · Amazon</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Price an AI assistant add-on for sellers on a marketplace so that it makes money.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Charge a flat monthly fee similar to competitors.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Computes cost per task and margin and considers heavy users.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes margin by segment, shows how an average hides losses, designs allowance and overage, routing and caps, tests price elasticity, and ties price to seller value.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real product. A flat fee is attractive to sellers and risky because AI cost scales with use.</p>\n<p><em>Cost by segment.</em> Suppose a seller's average use is 150 tasks a month at $0.04 each ($6) plus $1.50 of other cost: $7.50. A heavy seller (10% of sellers) runs 900 tasks: $36 plus $1.50: $37.50. At a flat $15 a month: average margin $7.50 (50%), heavy margin −$22.50. Blended cost: 0.9 × $7.50 + 0.1 × $37.50 = $10.50, so the margin is $4.50, 30%.</p>\n<p><em>Redesign.</em> Include 400 tasks a month and charge $0.05 per additional task. The heavy seller then pays $15 + 500 × $0.05 = $40 against a cost of $37.50: a small positive margin. Blended revenue: 0.9 × $15 + 0.1 × $40 = $17.50, margin $17.50 − $10.50 = $7.00, 40%. Average sellers see no change.</p>\n<p><em>Other levers.</em> Route simple tasks to cheaper models (cost per task falls; test quality by route); cache repeated outputs; shorten prompts; offer a higher-priced tier for power users; consider pricing by outcome where value is measurable (a listing that sells).</p>\n<p><em>Value anchor.</em> Price relative to what the seller gains: if the assistant saves a seller five hours a month or lifts conversion, a $15 fee is small; measure that benefit and use it in the message.</p>\n<p><em>Test.</em> Price experiments by cohort for elasticity (conversion, churn, usage), watch complaints about overage, and monitor margin by cohort monthly. Keep in mind the marketplace context: sellers who feel nickel-and-dimed may leave, so make allowances generous enough that most never see overage.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Amazon is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of pricing and unit-economics prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Amazon data or pricing is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if model prices fall by half next year? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Margins improve and competitors may cut prices, so I would not bank the savings in the price; I would keep the allowance structure, reinvest part of the saving in more included usage if the market demands it, and keep tracking cost per task. Plan on current costs and treat declines as upside.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you avoid sellers gaming the allowance? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Metering and rate limits per account, detection of automated resale of the assistant's output, and terms of use. Watch for accounts whose usage pattern looks like resale, and review heavy users to see whether they are valuable sellers or abusers.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c25-08\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C25-08 · Hard · ai · AI · Stripe</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> An AI agent handles payment disputes and refunds for support. How do you decide how much autonomy to give it, and when to give it more?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Let it handle small refunds on its own.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Sets approval levels by amount and error cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Defines promotion evidence with sample sizes, per-band limits, demotion triggers, audit and reversibility, and weighs the economics against the cost of error.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real system. Autonomy should be earned per action and per amount band, with evidence and a way back (FW-C25-05).</p>\n<p><em>Start low.</em> At first the agent drafts the decision and the reply; a person approves each one. This gathers data on how often the agent is right, for each type and amount.</p>\n<p><em>Promotion evidence.</em> To promote small refunds (under $20) to autonomous, require: at least 300 supervised cases in that band with no errors (the upper 95% bound on the error rate is then about 1%, by the rule of three), or about 600 for a 0.5% bound; no severe incident; low override rate by reviewers; reversibility (a refund can be reversed or limited); and monitoring and a kill switch in place. Each amount band and each case type (duplicate charge, item not received, fraud claim) earns its own evidence; reliability on $10 duplicate charges says little about $500 fraud disputes.</p>\n<p><em>Limits that bound the risk.</em> Caps on amount per refund and per customer per month, a daily total, a rule that suspicious patterns go to a person, an audit log of every action with the evidence used, and a short reversal window.</p>\n<p><em>Demotion.</em> A written rule: if the error rate in a week exceeds 1% on at least 100 cases, or any severe incident occurs (a refund to the wrong party, a fraud loss above a set amount), drop that band back to approval and investigate.</p>\n<p><em>Economics.</em> Suppose 50,000 refunds a month, and automating a refund saves $2 of handling: $100,000 a month. If 0.5% are wrong at an average cost of $30, errors cost 250 × $30 = $7,500 a month, a small fraction of the savings, so promotion pays when the evidence supports a 0.5% bound. For larger amounts the cost of an error rises with the amount, and the evidence bar rises with it.</p>\n<p><em>Fraud and abuse.</em> Refund agents attract manipulation (a customer's message instructing the agent); treat message text as untrusted, never let it change limits, and require extra checks for risky signals.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Stripe is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of automation-with-money prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Stripe data or practice is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the agent is very accurate but a rare error is severe? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Separate error types by severity: a high accuracy on routine errors does not allow autonomy where one error is catastrophic. Cap the amount so the worst case is bounded, require approval for the severe categories regardless of accuracy, and track severe errors with a zero-tolerance trigger.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you know reviewers are not rubber-stamping approvals? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Measure the override rate and the time per approval, seed the queue with known-bad cases to see whether reviewers catch them, and rotate reviewers. If almost nothing is overridden and seeded errors are missed, approvals are not adding safety, and the process needs redesign.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c25-09\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C25-09 · Hard · ai · AI</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Your company sells an AI product in several regions. How do you approach regulation and compliance?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Ask legal to check it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Classifies by risk and notes regional differences.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Triages each use by risk tier and market, uses current dates carefully (including deferrals), builds documentation and transparency into the product, and sets owners and launch implications, without giving legal advice.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I am not a lawyer and rules change quickly, so I'd involve legal and policy from the start and check current texts <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>. My job as PM is to give them a clear picture and build the product to meet what they decide.</p>\n<p><em>Triage (FW-C25-07).</em> List each AI use and each market. Classify by risk tier: prohibited uses; high-risk uses (employment decisions, credit, education, essential services, safety components); limited-risk uses with transparency duties (chatbots must disclose that the user is dealing with a machine; generated content may need labeling); minimal risk (a spam filter). Take an example: a customer-service chatbot is typically limited-risk, so the main duty is disclosure; a resume-screening feature is typically high-risk, with obligations on documentation, human oversight, data quality, and testing for bias.</p>\n<p><em>Know the dates, and read deferrals carefully.</em> In the European Union, for example, prohibited practices have applied since February 2025, obligations for general-purpose model providers since August 2025, and transparency duties for chatbots and generated content from 2 August 2026 (with a transition to 2 December 2026 for machine-readable marking by generative systems already on the market); the obligations for high-risk systems, originally due 2 August 2026, were deferred by Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 to 2 December 2027 for stand-alone systems and 2 August 2028 for AI embedded in regulated products <code>[V: Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 on EUR-Lex; legal summaries for the dates]</code>. A deferral is not an exemption: the work of documentation and testing takes time, and transparency duties may already apply.</p>\n<p><em>Build in what is required.</em> Disclosure that the user is interacting with an AI; labeling of generated content; human review for significant decisions; records of the system's purpose, data, evaluation results, known limits and monitoring (a model card or equivalent); logs for audit; data protection (what is collected, retention, user rights).</p>\n<p><em>Other regions.</em> In the United States, existing consumer-protection and anti-discrimination laws apply and some states and cities have AI-specific rules <code>[V: US: April 2023 joint statement by EEOC, CFPB, FTC and DOJ on existing law; NYC Local Law 144; other state rules not enumerated]</code>; in India, data protection law, non-binding AI governance guidelines and binding IT Rules amendments on synthetic content apply <code>[V: India: non-binding MeitY guidelines and binding IT Rules amendments, per law-firm summaries, 2026]</code>; in China, specific rules cover recommendation algorithms and generative services <code>[V: China: algorithm, deep synthesis and generative AI rules, per law-firm summaries]</code>. A feature may need different designs, or a delayed launch, in different markets.</p>\n<p><em>Operate it.</em> Assign owners (legal, policy, security, product), put compliance gates into the launch checklist, and review when rules change.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> A feature is high-risk in one market only. What do you do? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Decide with legal whether to launch there with the required controls, to launch a limited version (for example with a person making the final decision), or to delay that market; do not weaken the design everywhere by default, and do not ship a single global behavior that is non-compliant somewhere. Keep one codebase with market-specific configuration where possible.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you keep up with changing rules? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Name an owner (policy or legal) who tracks changes and a quarterly review with product; keep a register of AI uses with their tiers and obligations; and treat compliance requirements as inputs to the roadmap with lead time, not as last-minute gates.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c25-10\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C25-10 · Intermediate · ai · AI</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> What is the moat of an AI writing assistant if the underlying models keep improving?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Our AI is better than the competition's.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names data, distribution or workflow as sources of advantage.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Tests each source for durability, separates what compounds from what does not, notes that model access is not a moat, and says what to build toward.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd be honest: the model itself is unlikely to be the moat if others can access the same or better models. So I'd test each candidate source with the sources-of-advantage test (FW-C18-05).</p>\n<p><em>Weak sources.</em> A clever prompt (easy to copy and to replace); a thin wrapper around a general model; the choice of model (rivals can switch); novelty.</p>\n<p><em>Possible sources.</em> (1) <em>Distribution:</em> if the assistant sits inside the tools where people already write (email, documents), it reaches users that a standalone rival must win. (2) <em>Workflow integration and switching costs:</em> the more it holds a person's or team's templates, style guides, past documents and approvals, the harder it is to leave. (3) <em>Proprietary data and feedback loops:</em> data that others lack and that improves with use (what edits users make to suggestions, house style) can compound, though a stronger general model may erase the advantage if the task does not need your data. (4) <em>Evaluation capability:</em> a mature test set and monitoring let me improve faster and ship safely; a new entrant starts from nothing. (5) <em>Trust and accountability:</em> in domains with costly errors (legal, medical, finance), a record of reliability, audit trails and clear responsibility matters. (6) <em>Cost position:</em> routing and caching deliver the same quality for less, which allows a better price or margin.</p>\n<p><em>What compounds.</em> Workflow depth, data from use and trust compound; prompts and model choice do not. So I'd invest in becoming part of how teams write: shared style guides, review flows, integrations, and an eval that makes each model upgrade safe to adopt.</p>\n<p><em>What I'd watch.</em> Whether competitors with the same models match our quality; whether the platforms we depend on add the feature natively; and whether our retention comes from habit and integration or from novelty.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the platform you sit inside builds the same thing? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then the product must be better where the platform cannot or will not go: a specialized workflow, a vertical's compliance and data, deeper integration across several tools the platform does not span, or a different audience. Otherwise the honest answer is that the feature is a feature, not a company, and I would plan accordingly.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Does proprietary data always give a moat? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;No. If a general model can do the task well without your data, the advantage vanishes; if the data is hard to get, improves the product measurably as it grows, and is legal and consented to use, it can last. Test it: does a model with our data beat a strong general model by a margin users notice?&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c25-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Figures are assumptions unless marked, and the arithmetic was computed.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c25-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C25-01 · Size an eval set</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> You expect a 5% error rate and want it within ±2 points at 95% confidence. How many cases do you need? If you want to read five languages separately at that precision, how many in total? What can you say from a set of 30 cases with no errors?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Planning an evaluation.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> n = 1.96² × p × (1 − p) ÷ δ².</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C25-02.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> n = 3.84 × 0.05 × 0.95 ÷ 0.0004 = about 456. Five languages at that precision: about 2,280. With 30 cases and no errors, the 95% upper bound on the error rate is about 3 ÷ 30 = 10%: the set cannot show that the rate is low. To claim below 1% you need about 300 clean cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Accept a wider margin (±3 points) for low-traffic languages: n falls to about 203 each.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Tighter margins and more slices cost more reviewers' time; spend where errors cost most.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c25-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C25-02 · Agreement beyond chance</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Two reviewers label 200 outputs as bad or good. They agree both bad on 10, both good on 170; A says bad and B good on 10; A says good and B bad on 10. Compute raw agreement, chance agreement and kappa, and say what to do.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Checking a rubric.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Kappa = (observed − expected) ÷ (1 − expected).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C25.1 and Worked example 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Observed agreement: (10 + 170) ÷ 200 = 90%. Each reviewer flags 20 of 200 (10%), so expected agreement is 0.1 × 0.1 + 0.9 × 0.9 = 82%. Kappa = (0.90 − 0.82) ÷ 0.18 = 0.44: moderate. The rubric is ambiguous: review the 20 disagreements, add scored examples that settle them, retrain reviewers, and remeasure on a new sample. Do not rely on a 90% raw agreement.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Have a third reviewer break ties, and record the rationale to refine the rubric.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Rubric work costs time and reduces noise in every later measurement.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c25-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C25-03 · Calibrate a model grader</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Of 250 outputs, humans find 50 bad. A model grader flags 40, of which 30 are among the 50. Compute precision, recall and accuracy, and propose a triage design.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Using a model judge.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> True positives 30, false positives 10, false negatives 20, true negatives 190. Precision 30 ÷ 40 = 75%. Recall 30 ÷ 50 = 60%. Accuracy (30 + 190) ÷ 250 = 88%. The 88% hides that 40% of the bad outputs pass. Triage: humans review the 40 flagged plus a random sample (say 15%) of the 210 passed, to estimate the miss rate (20 of 210 is 9.5%); improve the grader with claim-by-claim checks and examples of subtle failures; recalibrate.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Use two different graders and send disagreements to humans.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> More human review raises cost and certainty; choose by the cost of a missed failure.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c25-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C25-04 · Acceptance and break-even</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A feature shows 500,000 suggestions; 40% are accepted. Each acceptance saves 30 seconds. 6% of accepted suggestions are wrong, and each wrong one costs 8 minutes. Compute the net time and the break-even error rate. What do you recommend?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> An online test readout.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 3.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Accepted: 200,000. Time saved: 200,000 × 0.5 minute = 100,000 minutes. Wrong accepted: 6% × 200,000 = 12,000; cost 12,000 × 8 = 96,000 minutes. Net: +4,000 minutes, barely positive. Break-even error rate: 0.5 ÷ 8 = 6.25%, so 6% is almost at break-even and uncertainty about the 6% (a sample of a few hundred has a wide interval) means the feature may be harmful. Recommend: improve the error rate (restrict contexts, highlight facts), re-measure with a larger sample, and require the interval's upper end to be below the break-even before launch.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Show suggestions only when the model is confident and measure the error rate in that subset.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Restricting suggestions lowers acceptance and raises net value.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c25-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C25-05 · Layer arithmetic</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> There are 200,000 attack attempts a day. An input filter stops 70%, the model's refusal stops 85% of what passes, and an output filter stops 60% of what remains. Compute successful attacks. Then compare improving the output filter to 80% against improving the input filter to 85%.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Safety planning.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Passing the input filter: 30%; passing refusal: 15% of those; passing the output filter: 40% of those. Success: 200,000 × 0.3 × 0.15 × 0.4 = 3,600 a day. Output filter at 80%: 200,000 × 0.3 × 0.15 × 0.2 = 1,800. Input filter at 85%: 200,000 × 0.15 × 0.15 × 0.4 = 1,800. Both halve the leakage; the better choice depends on cost and on the effect on legitimate users (over-refusal). Check each filter's false-positive rate on benign requests before choosing.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a rate limit that halves the attempts from repeat attackers, which halves all of the above.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Stricter filters block more legitimate use; measure both.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c25-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C25-06 · Unit economics</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A $15 per-user monthly AI add-on: the average user runs 200 tasks at $0.02 plus $1.50 other cost; heavy users (15% of users) run 1,000 tasks. Compute the margin for each segment and blended, and propose a change.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Pricing review.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C25-06.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Average user: cost 200 × $0.02 + $1.50 = $5.50; margin $9.50 (63%). Heavy user: 1,000 × $0.02 + $1.50 = $21.50; margin −$6.50. Blended cost: 0.85 × $5.50 + 0.15 × $21.50 = $4.675 + $3.225 = $7.90; blended margin $7.10 (47%). The heavy segment loses money and takes a quarter of the average user's margin. Options: include 500 tasks with overage at $0.03 (the heavy user pays $15 + 500 × $0.03 = $30, margin $8.50); route simple tasks to a cheaper model; a higher tier. Test elasticity before changing.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Raise the price to $18 for all; simpler and affects everyone.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Overage protects margin and creates friction; make allowances generous for typical users.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c25-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C25-07 · Read a regression by slice</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A suite of 500 cases (400 English, 100 Spanish) scores before a model update: English 384 pass (96%), Spanish 96 pass (96%). After: English 384 pass, Spanish 84 pass. Compute the overall pass rates and say what to do.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A launch gate.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> CS-C25-01.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Before: (384 + 96) ÷ 500 = 96%. After: (384 + 84) ÷ 500 = 93.6%. The overall figure fell 2.4 points, which could pass a gate set at 93%; but all of the loss is in Spanish, which fell from 96% to 84% (12 points). A per-slice gate (for example, no language below 92%) would block the release. Investigate why Spanish regressed (tokenization, prompt wording, retrieval of Spanish documents), fix, and rerun.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Ship the update for English only while Spanish is fixed, if the system can route by language.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Per-slice gates block more releases and prevent quiet harm to smaller groups.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c25-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C25-08 · A trust progression plan</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write promotion and demotion rules for letting an agent send automatic order-status updates and answer simple returns questions, and say how many supervised cases justify promotion if you want a 95% upper bound of 1% on the error rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Autonomy design.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Upper bound about 3 ÷ n for zero errors in n cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C25-05.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> For a 1% bound with no errors, n = 3 ÷ 0.01 = 300 supervised cases per category (about 600 for a 0.5% bound). Promotion rule: promote a category (order status; return-eligibility answers) when it has at least 300 supervised cases with no errors, no severe incident, a reviewer override rate under 2%, a reversal path, and monitoring and a kill switch in place. Demotion: if the weekly error rate exceeds 1% on at least 100 cases, or one severe incident occurs, return the category to approval and investigate. Limits: only the customer's own orders, no changes to payment or address, a daily action cap, and a log of every action.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Use a sequential test that allows earlier promotion if there are no errors and demotion if errors appear.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A higher evidence bar delays benefits; a lower one raises risk.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c25-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C25-09 · Compliance triage</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Classify these uses by likely risk tier under a risk-based regime such as the EU AI Act and say the main obligation: (a) a customer-service chatbot; (b) a resume-screening tool; (c) a spam filter; (d) a system that scores people's creditworthiness; (e) a tool that infers employees' emotions from video at work.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Product planning across markets.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C25-07.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <code>[V: EU AI Act as amended by Regulation (EU) 2026/1744]</code> (a) Limited risk: disclose that users are talking to an AI. (b) High-risk (employment): documentation, data quality, human oversight, testing for bias, and logs; applicable dates were deferred in the EU to December 2027, but preparation takes time. (c) Minimal risk: no special obligations. (d) High-risk (access to essential services such as credit): the same family of obligations as (b). (e) Likely prohibited in workplaces under the EU's rules on emotion recognition, with narrow medical or safety exceptions (the ban has applied since 2 February 2025 and covers recruitment) <code>[V: EU AI Act Article 5(1)(f)]</code>: do not build or deploy it there. Every classification must be confirmed with counsel and the current text.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> For (b) and (d), design for human decision-making with the model as one input, which also supports oversight duties.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Higher tiers add cost and delay; classification mistakes cost more.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c25-10\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C25-10 · A timed full answer</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Give a 30-minute answer to: &quot;Design the evaluation and launch plan for an AI agent that handles customer-support conversations for a bank.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Self-scored; record yourself. State assumptions aloud.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None. Label what you assume.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Use the 30-minute budget in section 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C25-08, scored against the six behaviors in section 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> An outline of a strong answer. Cost of error: wrong information about fees, deadlines or fraud steps creates liability and harm, and the company answers for what the agent says (C24, CS-C24-02). Eval: 1,000 real conversations (anonymized, with consent and legal review), sliced by topic (cards, loans, fraud, complaints), language and customer type, plus adversarial and unanswerable cases and scripted fraud attempts; rubric (supported by policy text, correct escalation, tone, no disclosure of other customers' data); two reviewers with a kappa target of at least 0.8; a calibrated model grader for triage; pass bar: at most 1% unsupported claims overall, none in fees and fraud steps, zero leaks, and 100% correct escalation of fraud and complaint cases in the test; per-language gates. Metrics: resolution without escalation, accepted-and-wrong (sampled), customer satisfaction, complaints, cost per conversation, time to resolution; guardrails on regulator-reportable events. Safety: layers (scope: no advice, no money movement; input filtering for injection; grounded answers quoting current policy; output checks for account data; limited permissions; human review queue; monitoring), red-team by harm category including social-engineering attempts, kill switch and incident owner.</p>\n<p>Autonomy: starts as draft for agents; promotes informational answers after 300 supervised cases per category with no errors; money movement stays with people. Economics: cost per conversation against the cost of a human-handled one; routing; heavy-case limits. Regulation: the AI-interaction disclosure, record keeping, bank regulators' expectations on model risk and complaints handling, data protection <code>[V: AI Act Article 50 as amended by Regulation (EU) 2026/1744; the other items were not rechecked]</code>. Rollout: shadow, then staff-facing, then a small customer group, with a long-term holdout on satisfaction and complaints.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Start with the assistant supporting human agents (draft replies and policy lookup) and only later face customers directly.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Agent assistance carries lower risk and lower savings than customer-facing automation.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for three weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: evaluation basics.</em> Use IQ-C25-01, IQ-C25-02, EX-C25-01, EX-C25-02 and EX-C25-03. Compute sample sizes and kappa by hand and say the eval design card aloud for a new feature.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: metrics, graders and design.</em> Use IQ-C25-04, IQ-C25-05, IQ-C25-06, EX-C25-04, EX-C25-05 and EX-C25-07. Compute a break-even and a leakage figure, and read a regression by slice.</li>\n<li><em>Week 3: autonomy, economics, regulation and full answers.</em> Use IQ-C25-03, IQ-C25-07, IQ-C25-08, IQ-C25-09, IQ-C25-10, EX-C25-06, EX-C25-08, EX-C25-09 and EX-C25-10. Run a full 30-minute answer with a partner who raises a failure or a regulation midway.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to say &quot;Acceptance is 45%,&quot; &quot;The judge agrees 90% of the time&quot; and &quot;We tested it on 50 examples&quot; at random. Practice answering with a break-even, recall on failures and a sample-size calculation.</p>\n<p><strong>Build a mini eval.</strong> For a task you know, write 30 cases and a three-line rubric, ask a colleague to score ten independently, and compute agreement beyond chance. Revise the rubric once and measure again.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I design the eval before the feature, with real, sliced and adversarial cases?</li>\n<li>Did I size the set and report intervals?</li>\n<li>Did I check reviewer agreement beyond chance and calibrate any model grader?</li>\n<li>Did I separate output quality from product behavior and measure accepted-and-wrong?</li>\n<li>Did I plan shadow mode, a staged launch and a holdout?</li>\n<li>Did I layer safety, red-team by category and say what a clean test proves?</li>\n<li>Did I set autonomy by evidence with limits and demotion rules?</li>\n<li>Did I compute cost per task and margin by segment?</li>\n<li>Did I classify risk and regulation by market, with current dates?</li>\n<li>Did I close with a decision and what would change it?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> For each drill record the date, the prompt, the pass bar and sample size you stated, the metric you distrusted, the safeguards, your score out of ten and the point you rushed.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter on real work over the next three weeks. If you do not work on AI features, apply it to an AI tool your team uses. Each deliverable doubles as an interview story.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Pick an AI feature or tool and write its eval design card (FW-C25-01): failure types, cases, rubric, judges and a pass bar tied to the cost of error. Review it with the engineer or a colleague and note what is missing.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Collect 50 real cases (including awkward ones). Score them yourself and with a colleague; compute raw agreement and kappa. Fix the rubric and remeasure.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Compute the sample size you would need for the rate and slices you care about, and compare it with what exists today.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> List the feature's metrics by layer (FW-C25-03) and identify the one that could mislead (for example, acceptance); design a way to measure accepted-and-wrong and compute a break-even.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Write the safety layers (FW-C25-04) for the feature, estimate each layer's share, and compute the leakage; name the kill switch owner.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 3.</strong> Build the unit-economics sheet by user segment (FW-C25-06), and the risk-tier note for the markets you serve (FW-C25-07), reviewed with finance and legal.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the eval design card with the kappa check (a time you made quality measurable), the break-even calculation (a time you distrusted a metric and quantified why), and the layered safety and economics notes (a time you designed for failure and for margin). Keep the numbers real, and keep what you did separate from what the team did.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c25-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Build the evaluation before the feature: real, sliced and adversarial cases, a rubric reviewers agree on beyond chance, a pass bar tied to the cost of error, run on every change and read by slice, with a hidden test set and a &quot;never again&quot; set from production.</li>\n<li>Size and read evaluations honestly: cases needed grow with precision and slices (n = 1.96² × p × (1 − p) ÷ δ²), small clean sets cannot certify low error rates (about 3 ÷ n), and a model grader must be calibrated on recall for real failures, not agreement.</li>\n<li>Separate model quality from product value and distrust acceptance: measure accepted-and-wrong and compute the break-even error rate from time saved and cost of error; read effects by week; keep a long-term holdout and use shadow mode.</li>\n<li>Design safety in layers and test it adversarially: multiply what each layer lets through, state what a clean red-team result proves, plan monitoring, a kill switch and incident response, and earn autonomy with evidence, limits and demotion rules.</li>\n<li>Make the product viable and lawful: compute cost per task by user segment (heavy users can turn margin into loss), classify uses by risk tier and market with current dates, keep documentation, and build defensibility from what compounds (workflow, data, trust, evaluation), not from a prompt or model choice.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c25-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Chip Huyen, <em>AI Engineering</em> (2025), for evaluation, monitoring and the economics of building on foundation models <code>[V: O'Reilly, first release 4 December 2024, in print January 2025, ISBN 9781098166304]</code>.</li>\n<li>Ron Kohavi, Diane Tang and Ya Xu, <em>Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments</em> (2020), for experiments with guardrails, novelty and long-term effects <code>[V: Cambridge University Press, 2 April 2020, ISBN 9781108724265; Twyman's law is chapter 3]</code>.</li>\n<li>Peter Lee, &quot;Learning from Tay's introduction,&quot; Official Microsoft Blog (25 March 2016; blogs.microsoft.com/blog/2016/03/25/learning-tays-introduction/). The primary source for CS-C25-02.</li>\n<li>Microsoft's Responsible AI Standard and guidelines for human-AI interaction, and similar published frameworks from other companies, as examples of governance practice <code>[V: Microsoft Responsible AI Standard]</code>.</li>\n<li>The text of the EU AI Act and the 2026 amending package, and official guidance from the European Commission, read for current dates (not legal advice) <code>[V: Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 on EUR-Lex]</code>.</li>\n<li>The NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF 1.0, NIST AI 100-1, January 2023, with a Generative AI Profile, NIST AI 600-1, July 2024), as a structured approach to identifying and managing AI risks <code>[V: NIST AI 100-1, 26 January 2023; Generative AI Profile NIST AI 600-1, 26 July 2024; NIST's page says AI RMF 1.0 is being revised, checked October 2026]</code>.</li>\n<li>Margaret Mitchell and colleagues, &quot;Model Cards for Model Reporting,&quot; <em>Proceedings of FAT</em> '19* (2019), pp. 220 to 229, on documenting a model's intended use and evaluation <code>[V: FAT* '19, 29 to 31 January 2019, pp. 220 to 229, doi 10.1145/3287560.3287596]</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"part-viii\"></span>\n";
